{
  "dom_id": "step-q118474",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,000y-10{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$",
  "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200098/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=10y(100-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=100.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $100.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=100.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
